def test_network_services_fetch_wiring_read_only(zone):
    """Read-wiring check for the network_services idempotency opt-in (Batch 6 rollout).

    SAFETY — READ-ONLY on purpose, and emphatically so: network_services
    configures REAL routing, VPN, and interfaces (zones, IKE/IPsec gateways and
    tunnels, BGP/OSPF routers, NAT/PBF rules, SD-WAN paths, physical/logical
    interfaces). A create -> apply -> absent quartet against any of these would
    stand up and tear down live routing/VPN/interface configuration on the shared
    tenant, so it is categorically unsafe. The generic apply/absent path is
    already live-proven on objects and device_settings through the same engine
    and strategies, so this batch (the largest — 47 opted-in resources) verifies
    only the resource-shape-specific READ half: fetch() drives the wired
    list_scan (the folder/snippet/device-scoped paginated bare-array scan +
    identity match) against a live folder, deserializing every real zone the
    tenant lists through from_dict — the exact failure mode a faulty generated
    model produces.

    Targets `zone` specifically: it is a safe, read-only-to-fetch, folder-scoped
    full-CRUD resource that inherits the subpackage folder/snippet/device scope
    default (unlike the scope-less auto_vpn_cluster and the auto_vpn_setting
    singleton, which override the default to an empty scope).

    network_services objects live in deployment folders, not the object folder
    `Shared`; which folders exist is tenant-specific. So a server-reached
    ApiException (e.g. a 400 "folder doesn't exist") still proves the fetch WIRING
    built and sent a well-formed scoped request — the failure this guards against,
    a model that can't deserialize a real object, surfaces as a ValidationError
    from from_dict, which is NOT caught here and still fails the test."""
    try:
        missing = zone.fetch(name=_name(), folder="Remote Networks")
    except ApiException as exc:
        # request reached the server and came back well-formed (wiring proven);
